{
  "analysis": "The review describes a wheel-based chance mechanic in the game's genie game. The wheel appears to stop on a valuable prize, but the genie then forces another spin and the resulting prize is much lower. This is both a variable reward delivery system and an apparent manipulation of the outcome after the player believes the result has been determined. The review does not describe spending real money or paid currency on the spin, so Gambling and Intermediate Currency are not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "the wheel spin after stopping on a great price",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics / Variable Rewards indicator",
      "rationale": "A wheel spin delivers rewards through an unpredictable chance-based mechanism."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "after stopping on a great price the genie move the wheel to a spin tag",
      "rule_applied": "definition; alleged rigging boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The game is described as changing the wheel's outcome after it has apparently stopped, implying that the result is manipulated rather than determined by the player's apparent timing or control."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review primarily attributes the described problems to bugs, crashing, lag, unresponsive controls, and freezing after an update. Under R11, those technical faults do not themselves receive dark-pattern labels. The review also explicitly identifies PvP battles, which are competitive gameplay between players; this underlying mechanic remains codable even though the reviewer says it is malfunctioning. Trading and gifts with friends are mentioned, but no obligation to reciprocate or social pressure is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the pvp & rockets battles",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes PvP battles, a game mode in which players compete against one another; the accompanying lag is a technical fault and does not erase the underlying competition mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a 30-second matchmaking connection wait, but the codebook excludes service-operation waits such as matchmaking queues from Playing by Appointment. It also describes weapon upgrades and perks as difficult to obtain, with the player having to keep battling to win; this implies repeated gameplay activity is required for advancement, supporting Grinding. The complaint that rewards are merely 'for show' is too vague to identify a specific reward-delivery mechanic, and no payment, purchase, or explicit opponent relationship is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "Its difficult to get weapon upgrades or any percs others you stick battling to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Grinding boundary rule: assign where the span identifies the repeated activity required for progress.",
      "rationale": "The span implies that the player must keep battling repeatedly to obtain upgrades or perks and achieve wins."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes competitive matches against other players, with repeated use of Mega Knight, P.E.K.K.A., and Golem shaping the opposing decks. It explicitly characterizes the game as pay to win and says advancement is not achievable through skill alone, which describes paid advantage affecting progress or competitive success. The complaints about updates and card buffs describe disliked balance changes, but do not state that a purchased item lost value, so Power Creep is not supported. The annoying card strategy is ordinary competitive play rather than a design incentive for antisocial behavior.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "all you play against after 8k trophies is mega knight,pekka, and golem",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player describes repeatedly competing against opposing decks in matches."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "its also pay to win",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes spending money as granting an advantage needed to win or advance competitively."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a paid pass, which external search resolves as Fishdom's paid seasonal/Gold Pass. The reviewer recommends not buying anything beyond that pass, so the pass mechanic is present even though they approve of limiting purchases. The review also explicitly describes trading lives with friends and teammates, which is a resource exchange with existing players. The missing game element and the customer-service failure are reported as an issue or failed support outcome, not as a deliberate dark-pattern mechanic. The statement that the game does not require payment to win does not establish pay-to-progress because it denies that payment is necessary.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_RecurringFee",
      "span": "beyond the pass",
      "rule_applied": "Battle passes are an indicator of Recurring Fee; R10 search resolved Fishdom's pass as a paid seasonal/Gold Pass.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer identifies a paid pass as an in-game purchase mechanic, which is covered by the battle-pass indicator."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Reciprocity",
      "span": "Get friends and a team and trade lives",
      "rule_applied": "Reciprocity indicator: feeling obligated to return a favor or share resources; the boundary rule assigns this when players exchange resources such as lives.",
      "rationale": "Trading lives describes an exchange of in-game resources with other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Fishdom \"pass\" paid pass game",
  "search_result": "The search established that Fishdom's pass is a paid seasonal/Gold Pass that provides special rewards. ([playrix.helpshift.com](https://playrix.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-fishdom/faq/4134-season-challenges-1757580206/?f=how-do-i-refill-lives-how-many-lives-can-i-have&s=match-3-levels&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two active mechanics. First, lives are replenished through a wait, as shown by the request for a shorter interval before receiving a new life; this implies an in-game timer that restricts continued play. Second, daily prizes use a daily-return system that resets the player's accumulated reward progress when a day is missed. The comments about levels becoming harder and about small celebration tasks are evaluations or suggestions without a sufficiently specific dark-pattern mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "maybe only 5-10 minutes for a new life",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that impose an arbitrary wait before gameplay can continue; the suggestion presupposes an existing life-regeneration timer.",
      "rationale": "The request for a shorter time to receive a new life implies that the game currently makes the player wait for life regeneration before continuing to play."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "just because you miss a day doesnt mean that you need to start over on your daily prizes",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards: daily login incentives with an active consequence for missing a day, including a broken or reset reward streak.",
      "rationale": "The game provides daily prizes and resets the player's accumulated daily-reward progress when they miss a day."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement that cannot be dismissed normally: there is no close control, tapping the ad redirects to the store, returning to the game leaves the ad screen active, and the player must terminate the game to exit. The absent close option and forced store redirection obstruct the player's opt-out and direct attention toward the store, supporting UI Misdirection. Because the player is compelled to remain in or forcibly exit an ad rather than freely dismissing it, the ad mechanic also supports Advertisement. The review does not describe a purchase being completed or a direct insufficient-funds buying prompt, so Easy to Purchase is not assigned.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "after seeing the ads, there is no way to close it",
      "rule_applied": "Advertisement definition: compelled to view advertisements; the span describes an ad that cannot be dismissed.",
      "rationale": "The player is forced to remain on the advertisement screen because it has no normal close option."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "No cross mark on the screen , if you try to click anywhere on the screen it takes you to store",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary rule: assign when the interface obstructs an opt-out or exit control, or routes interaction toward a selected option.",
      "rationale": "The missing close control prevents opting out, while tapping the screen redirects the player to the store."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes accumulated in-game purchases and progress that would be lost if the game were deleted, making them reluctant to leave because their prior investment would be forfeited. The request to add saving is hypothetical and is not coded as an existing mechanic. The conditional statement about possibly spending money in the future does not establish a current payment mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_EasyToGetHardToLose",
      "span": "if I deleted the game I would lose everything my in-game purchases and my hard work I really don't want that to happen",
      "rule_applied": "Invested (Endowed) Value: accumulated time, money, and progress make abandoning the game difficult because leaving forfeits value already held.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer is reluctant to delete the game because doing so would sacrifice purchased items and hard-earned progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes building and expanding an empire and battling other players in real-time combat. Building and expansion are ordinary gameplay mechanics and do not match a listed dark-pattern label. The real-time battles explicitly place players in competition with one another, supporting S_Competition. No monetization, scheduling, reward, collection, social-pressure, or technical-storage mechanic is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "the thrill of battling other players in real-time combat",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes the game making players compete against other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes progression being blocked by a lack of extra energy and says purchasing is necessary to make any progress. This is a payment-based progression gate. The review does not describe a timer or energy regeneration schedule, a named premium currency, a purchase prompt, or any other qualifying mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "to have any change at progressing you need to purchase.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that progressing requires a purchase, indicating that continued advancement is contingent on payment."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy system that limits continued progress: once the player's energy is depleted, they cannot meaningfully advance until a recovery timer restores it. The long recovery timer is an in-game wait imposed by the design. The review does not describe paying to bypass the wait, repetitive tasks, daily rewards, or an unfinishable game.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers impose arbitrary waits, including energy or lives refilling over time; the boundary rule includes a wait or regeneration schedule that gates progress.",
      "rationale": "Depleted energy stops meaningful progress and the player must wait for the recovery timer before continuing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a paid consumable-item loop: after purchasing Poké Balls, captures allegedly fail more often, causing the balls to be depleted faster and requiring additional purchases. This is presented as outcome manipulation intended to create a payment-dependent path to continued capturing, supporting Illusion of Control and Pay to Progress. The edit separately claims that the original review and its statement about consulting other players were not written by the account holder, which describes the game posting a review under the player's identity and supports Friend Spam / Impersonation under the destination-of-message rule. No web search is needed because the mechanics are identifiable from the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "since I started purchasing, items \"poke\" balls that when I tried to make a capture that they keep breaking out",
      "rule_applied": "Alleged rigging boundary for Illusion of Control",
      "rationale": "The reviewer attributes repeated capture failures to a change associated with purchasing, implying that the game manipulates outcome determination to make the player use more balls."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Free alternative boundary",
      "rationale": "The consumable Poké Balls are needed for capturing, and the reviewer says continued play requires purchasing additional balls."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "I DID NOT WRITE THIS REVIEW.....I DIDN'T TALK TO SEVERAL PLAYERS BECAUSE I DO NOT KNOW ANYONE",
      "rule_applied": "Destination of the message rule",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the installed game posted a store review under their account and fabricated statements in their identity."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes progress being blocked by difficult levels and tasks that the player says are impossible without buying boosters. The week-long effort to pass hard levels indicates slow or difficult progress, but it does not specify a repetitive activity, an in-game timer, or a required play schedule, so it does not support Grinding or Playing by Appointment. The explicit paid alternative—buying boosters to overcome otherwise impossible tasks—supports Pay to Progress.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Free alternative boundary rule",
      "rationale": "The review states that completing tasks is effectively unavailable unless the player purchases boosters, making payment the route to progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a time-limited event with a countdown and a reward tied to completing the remaining three steps. The player ran out of dice before completion, returned after sleeping while the event supposedly still had two days remaining, and found that the event had changed, forfeiting the near-completed reward opportunity. This is a game-imposed schedule that required progress within a particular event window. The review does not describe a daily login reward, a payment actually made or required, a currency shortfall caused by item pricing, or explicit anxiety about missing out beyond the event schedule itself.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "Reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule and the loss; Playing by Appointment",
      "rationale": "The player had to complete the event within its stated real-world time window, but returning later resulted in the event changing before the reward was obtained."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a referral mechanic in which inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook is supposed to produce rewards, even though the reviewer says the rewards were not credited. It also describes luck-based play using accumulated dice at a maximum bet, with shut downs and bank heists as possible outcomes; this supports Gambling because an in-game stake and chance are both present. Finally, repeated duplicate stickers describe unwanted outcomes from a variable reward or randomized collection-delivery system, supporting Reward Mania. The inability to log in is explicitly attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a technical fault and does not receive a dark-pattern label under R11.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme indicator: rewards for inviting friends to join the game",
      "rationale": "The span describes a referral incentive tied to recruiting friends, even though the reviewer says the reward failed to appear."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists.",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling definition and boundary rule permitting an in-game currency stake without a purchase being described",
      "rationale": "The player uses accumulated dice in a maximum-bet, luck-based activity whose possible outcomes include shut downs and bank heists."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way...",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards indicator; duplicate or unwanted drops from a randomized reward pool",
      "rationale": "Repeated duplicate stickers describe an unpredictable reward-delivery outcome that keeps producing unwanted collection items."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a deliberate interface behavior in which opening the game or entering the 1v1 area automatically moves the player to the highest-stakes table affordable with their coins and selects it. This directs the player toward a costly option and is said to cause accidental mis-clicks. The resulting loss of all accumulated coins creates pressure to purchase more currency with real money, so the review also describes both payment being used to continue playing and real money being converted into in-game coins. The mention of a 1v1 section explicitly describes competition against another player. The requested Default Table Lock and lowest-table default are hypothetical fixes and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "Every time you open the game or go to the 1v1 section, it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "Exciting UI Effect / UI Misdirection boundary: assign where the interface directs the player toward a selected option or makes an unintended selection likely.",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-bet option, directing attention and interaction toward a costly choice."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Progress definition and Free alternative boundary: payment is presented as necessary after an in-game currency loss to continue playing.",
      "rationale": "The review says players are forced to pay for replacement currency after losing their coins, making payment the route to continued play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Intermediate Currency boundary: the currency is the object acquired through a purchase, even when the real-money amount is not specified.",
      "rationale": "The span describes purchasing replacement in-game coins with real money."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "go to the 1v1 section",
      "rule_applied": "Competition definition: the game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "A 1v1 section describes a mode in which the player competes directly against another player."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a perceived outcome-manipulation mechanic: the random number generation is said to work against the player, which implies that results are not fairly random. It also describes an unfavorable progression economy at about level 100, where entering a level costs 2200 and winning returns barely that amount, but it does not clearly state that payment is required to advance, that real money buys the 2200 currency, or that the level is a wager for a prize. The complaint that play without spending is not rewarding is evaluative and does not independently establish Pay to Progress, Gambling, Intermediate Currency, or Reward Mania. No web search is needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you",
      "rule_applied": "Alleged rigging boundary rule; the span asserts that the game manipulates or determines outcomes against the player rather than presenting ordinary randomness.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer claims the RNG is deliberately unfavorable, describing concealed or manipulated outcome determination."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an event whose outcome is effectively gated by spending: it calls the game pay to play and pay to win, then says 30k was spent to win the last event. This supports Pay to Progress because money is used to access or win event content. The phrase \"same old same old grinder\" is only a general evaluation of repetition and does not name a repeated task required for progress, so it does not support Grinding. No clear chance mechanic, currency conversion, daily schedule, collection pressure, or sunk-cost pressure is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to play. Zero skill pay to win. Calculated total spent on last event was 30k to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall and Pay to Win indicators under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review states that playing or winning the event requires substantial spending, including 30k spent to win."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several active game mechanics. Base upgrades impose increasingly long update or construction timers, creating a wait-to-play schedule. Progression also appears to scale without an attainable endpoint: everything keeps scaling and the player is left with only more grind, which supports an infinite treadmill but not Grinding because no specific repetitive activity is named. The review explicitly describes a pay-to-win structure, meaning spending provides gameplay advantages. It also attributes deliberate confusion and overload in the UI to pushing purchases, which supports UI Misdirection, but not Easy to Purchase because no specific purchase prompt, default, missing confirmation, or insufficient-funds interception is described. Finally, the alliance system creates adversarial player interaction, as unaffiliated players are attacked and bullied, supporting Competition. The alliance pressure does not support Forced Fellowship because the review describes coercive grouping for protection, not recruitment or an obligation to avoid letting teammates down.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes a monetized structure in which spending gives players gameplay advantages."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play indicator: in-game timers that impose arbitrary waits, including build timers",
      "rationale": "Base development is gated by increasingly long timers, requiring the player to wait according to the game's schedule."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: progression or goals continually expand without an attainable end state",
      "rationale": "The span describes endlessly scaling progression with no meaningful endpoint to work toward."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary: deliberate interface design that directs or obstructs players in service of a purchase objective",
      "rationale": "The reviewer attributes the confusing and overwhelming interface directly to steering players toward buying."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "Definition: patterns that make players compete against other players",
      "rationale": "The alliance system places players in adversarial relations where unaffiliated players are attacked by others."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily-reward system that requires the player to complete a wagering threshold: the daily rewards cannot be collected unless the player bets 50,000. This supports Daily Rewards because the game incentivizes activity within a daily cycle and withholds the daily reward when the condition is not met. The required bet is also a chance-based casino action, so Gambling applies even though the review does not say that the wager itself uses real money; the codebook permits Gambling when an in-game currency stake is wagered and no purchase is described. The mention of timed rewards only gives their amount and does not establish a separate reward-delivery manipulation.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards definition and boundary rule: a daily reward is withheld unless the player completes the required activity.",
      "rationale": "The daily reward system imposes a daily wagering requirement, so failing to meet it prevents completion and collection of the daily rewards."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "unless I bet 50,000",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling; the boundary rule permits this label when an in-game currency stake is wagered even without a described real-money purchase.",
      "rationale": "The player must stake 50,000 in a chance-based casino-style wager to qualify for the reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes monetized success and collection completion. It says that winning anything requires spending hundreds of dollars, which directly describes payment as the route to advantage or success. It also describes sticker sets whose final one or two items are effectively unavailable unless the player pays substantially, implying both a collection-completion pressure and developer-imposed scarcity tied to purchase. The statement that a goal succeeds only about one time in one hundred expresses poor odds, but it does not identify a chance-based reward system or a wager, so it is insufficient for Gambling or Reward Mania. The review-gating complaint concerns submitting a store review and does not match any listed in-game mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "if you want to win anything on this game it will cost hundreds of dollars",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator under M_PayToProgress",
      "rationale": "The reviewer says real-money spending is required to win or succeed in the game."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "on the stickers you will never get the last one or two",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reference to obtaining the last stickers describes pressure to complete a sticker collection."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "you will never get the last one or two unless you pay a lot of money to get them",
      "rule_applied": "Artificial Scarcity; scarcity bears directly on purchase",
      "rationale": "The final stickers are described as deliberately scarce or unavailable through ordinary play, with payment presented as the way to obtain them."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an existing competitive interaction in which another player can challenge the reviewer to a game. It also mentions purchasing a booster, but does not describe a monetary conversion, a purchase prompt, or payment being required for progress. Trading stickers is requested as a missing feature, so it is not coded under R12. The Facebook reference describes who is not using the platform, not the game sending messages or requesting access.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "another player challenge me to a game",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes players being matched against one another in a competitive game."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes progression becoming slow and repetitive: they repeatedly exhaust lives while trying to beat alternate levels, with each level taking an average of 5–7 days. They also state that buying add-ons is necessary to get through the game, making payment the alternative to the difficult free progression path. The daily count describes how often lives are exhausted, not a required login schedule or explicit regeneration timer, so it does not establish Playing by Appointment. The statement about wasting money and time while uninstalling does not by itself show that accumulated investment is making departure difficult.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I run out of lives 3-4 times daily to beat every alternate level, avg 5-7 days per level.",
      "rule_applied": "Grinding definition and boundary vs Slow Progress",
      "rationale": "Repeatedly exhausting lives while attempting levels describes tedious repeated attempts required for progression."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "You must buy add ons else you can't make it through",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The review states that continued advancement is unavailable unless the player purchases add-ons."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
